/* JAMB 2027 Prep — Use of English, Chapters 1–5 (Section A skills + vocabulary) */

JAMB.addChapter('english', {
  id: 'comprehension-skills',
  title: 'Comprehension: Reading the Passage',
  blurb: 'The five question types, the three-pass technique, and how to keep every answer inside the passage.',
  time: '50 min',
  objectives: [
    'Recognise the five question types set on comprehension passages',
    'Find the main idea of a passage or paragraph',
    'Answer detail and NOT questions by locating evidence in the text',
    'Infer meaning from context without importing outside knowledge',
    'Avoid the standard distractor traps'
  ],
  body: `
<p class="lead">The comprehension passage is worth more marks than any other single item on the Use of English paper, and it is the only part that cannot be crammed. The good news: <strong>every answer is already on the page in front of you.</strong> Comprehension is a search exercise wearing the costume of a reading exercise.</p>

<h2>What the paper looks like</h2>
<p>The UTME sets a passage of roughly 200 words drawn from an academic discipline — science, agriculture, history, economics, sociology — followed by questions. The passage type may be <strong>descriptive, narrative, expository</strong> or <strong>argumentative</strong>, but the questions draw from a fixed menu of five kinds:</p>

<table class="ledger" style="margin:1.2em 0">
  <thead><tr><th>Question type</th><th>Asks</th><th>Where the answer lives</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><b>Main idea / topic</b></td><td>What is the passage mainly about?</td><td>The first sentence, plus repeated nouns</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Detail / retrieval</b></td><td>What does the passage state?</td><td>An exact sentence — find and match</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Vocabulary in context</b></td><td>What does this word mean <em>here</em>?</td><td>The sentence around it, plus the paragraph’s mood</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Inference / deduction</b></td><td>What follows but is not stated?</td><td>A small step beyond a stated fact</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>NOT true / Except</b></td><td>Which option is NOT supported?</td><td>Three options will be stated; the odd one out is not</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="note note-exam"><span class="ico">🎯</span><div><b>Read the question before you hunt</b>
<p>On the CBT screen you see one question at a time with the passage above it. Read the <strong>question first</strong>, then go to the passage looking for that specific information. Reading the whole passage carefully, then again for each question, wastes the clock.</p></div></div>

<h2>The three-pass technique</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Skim (30 seconds).</strong> Read only the first sentence of each paragraph. You now know the passage’s skeleton and roughly where each answer will be.</li>
<li><strong>Hunt.</strong> For each question, read the question, then scan for the line that contains the answer. The questions follow the passage in order — answers move down the page with you.</li>
<li><strong>Check the words.</strong> The correct option will usually reuse the passage’s own vocabulary, paraphrased. If your option shares no word with the passage, be suspicious.</li>
</ol>

<h2>The traps</h2>
<div class="formula-box">
  <div class="fb-head">How distractors are built</div>
  <div class="formula-row"><div class="fname">True but not here</div>
    <div class="fexpr">A statement that is generally sensible but never appears in the passage</div></div>
  <div class="formula-row"><div class="fname">Half right</div>
    <div class="fexpr">Correct for the first clause, wrong for the second — read whole options</div></div>
  <div class="formula-row"><div class="fname">Extreme wording</div>
    <div class="fexpr">Words like <em>all, never, always, only, completely</em> that overstate a careful passage</div></div>
  <div class="formula-row"><div class="fname">The dictionary meaning</div>
    <div class="fexpr">On vocabulary questions, the commonest meaning is usually a distractor — the passage’s sense wins</div></div>
</div>

<h2>Practice passage</h2>
<div class="example">
  <div class="example-h"><span class="num">A</span> Read the passage, then answer the questions that follow
  </div>
  <div class="example-b">
    <div class="step"><div class="step-l">Passage</div>
    <p style="margin-top:.6em">The decline of soil fertility across much of West Africa has been described as a slow emergency. Farmers who once harvested generous crops from the same plots now watch their yields fall season after season. The causes are well known: continuous cropping without rest, the burning of crop residue, and erosion that strips the topsoil after every heavy rain. What is less widely understood is why the problem persists. Modern fertiliser remains expensive for the smallholder, and the organic alternatives — compost, rotation, long fallow periods — demand either labour or land that families cannot spare. Extension officers, whose duty is to carry improved methods into the villages, are few, and their transport is unreliable. In that gap tradition fills in: practices handed down through generations continue, not because farmers reject change, but because change carries risks they cannot afford. The consequence is a quiet deterioration of the resource on which everything else depends. Reversing it will require more than imported technology; it will require policies that make conservation cheaper than exhaustion.</p></div>
    <div class="step"><div class="step-l">Worked example</div>
    <b>Question:</b> The passage suggests that farmers keep old methods mainly because —
    <p style="margin-top:.5em"><b>A.</b> they do not know better · <b>B.</b> new methods are too risky for them · <b>C.</b> fertiliser is free · <b>D.</b> extension officers approve</p></div>
    <div class="step"><div class="step-l">Method</div>
    The line reads “…not because farmers reject change, but because change carries risks they cannot afford.” Option A contradicts the line; C and D are false elsewhere in the passage. <b>B</b> restates it.</div>
    <div class="ans">B — always match the option to an actual sentence</div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="note note-warn"><span class="ico">⚠</span><div><b>Never answer from your own knowledge</b>
<p>Passages are chosen so that outside knowledge is irrelevant — sometimes even misleading. If you find yourself thinking “but I read that fertiliser is being subsidised now”, that thought is worth zero marks. The passage is the world for the duration of the question.</p></div></div>

<div class="note note-tip"><span class="ico">💡</span><div><b>Opinion words signal the writer’s position</b>
<p>Watch for <em>however, yet, unfortunately, arguably, it seems</em>. These mark the turn where the writer’s real view appears — and main-idea and tone questions are usually answered at exactly that turn.</p></div></div>

<h2>Chapter recap</h2>
<ul>
<li>Five question types: main idea, detail, vocabulary in context, inference, NOT.</li>
<li>Skim first, hunt second — the questions run down the passage in order.</li>
<li>Correct options paraphrase the passage; distractors are true elsewhere, half-right, or overstated.</li>
<li>On vocabulary questions the passage’s sense beats the dictionary.</li>
<li>Everything you need is on the screen. Nothing needs to be memorised.</li>
</ul>
`,
  quiz: [
    { q: 'In the passage, the phrase “a slow emergency” suggests a problem that is', opts: ['Sudden and violent', 'Gradual but serious', 'Mild and temporary', 'Already solved'], a: 1, why: '“Slow” gives the pace and “emergency” the seriousness — gradual yet grave.' },
    { q: 'According to the passage, which of these is NOT given as a cause of falling yields?', opts: ['Continuous cropping', 'Burning of crop residue', 'Over-irrigation', 'Erosion of topsoil'], a: 2, why: 'Irrigation is never mentioned; the three stated causes are cropping, burning and erosion.' },
    { q: 'The writer says the causes of the decline are', opts: ['Complicated and disputed', 'Well known', 'Secret', 'Unimportant'], a: 1, why: '“The causes are well known…”' },
    { q: 'Extension officers are mentioned to show that', opts: ['Farmers ignore advice', 'Expert help is scarce and unreliable', 'Officers are well paid', 'Fertiliser is their duty'], a: 1, why: 'They are “few” and their transport “unreliable” — help exists but does not arrive.' },
    { q: 'From the passage, the smallholder cannot easily use modern fertiliser because it is', opts: ['Dangerous', 'Expensive', 'Unavailable abroad', 'Unfashionable'], a: 1, why: '“Modern fertiliser remains expensive for the smallholder.”' },
    { q: 'The writer’s main attitude towards the farmers is one of', opts: ['Contempt', 'Understanding', 'Amusement', 'Indifference'], a: 1, why: 'The writer defends them: they continue “not because they reject change” but because they cannot afford the risk.' },
    { q: '“A quiet deterioration” (last sentence) means', opts: ['A sudden collapse', 'A gradual decline', 'A noisy failure', 'A measured improvement'], a: 1, why: 'Quiet = unnoticed; deterioration = getting worse.' },
    { q: 'The passage implies that imported technology alone will', opts: ['Solve the problem', 'Not solve the problem', 'Make fertiliser cheaper', 'Increase erosion'], a: 1, why: '“Reversing it will require <em>more than</em> imported technology.”' },
    { q: 'The word “exhaustion” as used here refers to', opts: ['Tiredness of the farmer', 'Complete depletion of the soil', 'Overwork of the officials', 'Failure of the harvest'], a: 1, why: 'Conservation is contrasted with using the soil up — depletion.' },
    { q: 'The best summary of the passage is that soil decline continues because', opts: ['Farmers refuse to learn', 'Cheap alternatives are missing and support fails', 'The climate has changed', 'Too much fertiliser is sold'], a: 1, why: 'Costly fertiliser, labour-hungry organic options and scarce officers leave tradition in control.' },
    { q: 'The main idea of a passage is best found by', opts: ['Reading the last sentence only', 'Reading the first sentence of each paragraph and noting repeated nouns', 'Counting adjectives', 'Choosing the longest option'], a: 1, why: 'Topic sentences plus repeated vocabulary reveal what the passage is actually about.' },
    { q: 'On a “Which of the following is NOT true” question, the correct option is', opts: ['The one supported by the passage', 'The one the passage does not support', 'The shortest one', 'The one containing a number'], a: 1, why: 'Three options will be stated; the answer is the unsupported one.' },
    { q: 'The safest way to answer a vocabulary-in-context question is to', opts: ['Use the commonest dictionary meaning', 'Substitute each option into the sentence and check meaning and tone', 'Choose the longest word', 'Choose the rarest word'], a: 1, why: 'The option that fits the sentence’s sense and mood is the answer.' },
    { q: 'An inference question requires you to', opts: ['Repeat a sentence from the passage', 'Take one small step beyond what is stated', 'Add outside knowledge', 'Guess the writer’s biography'], a: 1, why: 'Inference is a deduction from the text, not beyond its evidence.' },
    { q: 'If a correct option shares no vocabulary with the passage, you should', opts: ['Accept it at once', 'Be suspicious and re-check the passage', 'Choose it anyway', 'Skip the question forever'], a: 1, why: 'Correct answers usually paraphrase actual words in the passage.' },
    { q: 'The word “however” in a passage usually signals', opts: ['A repetition', 'A contrast or turn in the argument', 'An example', 'The end of the passage'], a: 1, why: 'Contrast markers locate the writer’s real position — favourite material for tone questions.' },
    { q: 'In the practice passage, farmers continue old practices', opts: ['Because they reject new ideas', 'Although they would welcome change', 'Because the government insists', 'Only in the dry season'], a: 1, why: 'They continue “not because farmers reject change, but because change carries risks they cannot afford.”' },
    { q: 'A statement that is generally sensible but appears nowhere in the passage is', opts: ['Always the answer', 'A distractor', 'The writer’s view', 'An inference'], a: 1, why: 'General truth without textual support earns nothing.' },
    { q: 'The passage describes the decline as one on which “everything else depends”, showing that soil is', opts: ['One factor among many', 'Fundamental to everything else', 'Replaceable', 'Mainly a concern of scientists'], a: 1, why: 'That phrase marks it as the base resource.' },
    { q: 'The writer proposes that the solution must', opts: ['Come from abroad', 'Include policies favouring conservation', 'Increase borrowing', 'Abandon tradition entirely'], a: 1, why: 'The final sentence calls for “policies that make conservation cheaper than exhaustion.”' }
  ]
});

JAMB.addChapter('english', {
  id: 'cloze-passages',
  title: 'Cloze Passages: Filling the Gaps',
  blurb: 'Why each gap is a grammar or collocation question in disguise, and how to read the whole passage first.',
  time: '45 min',
  objectives: [
    'Understand how a cloze passage is constructed and marked',
    'Identify the part of speech each gap requires',
    'Use the passage’s tense, number and voice to fix verb gaps',
    'Use collocation and meaning to fix vocabulary gaps'
  ],
  body: `
<p class="lead">A cloze passage is a normal paragraph with numbered gaps punched through it. Candidates fear it because it feels like ten separate questions — but it is really <strong>one paragraph that tells you the answer to every gap</strong>. The passage carries its own grammar, and your job is to listen to it.</p>

<h2>Two kinds of gap</h2>
<div class="formula-box">
  <div class="fb-head">Gap categories</div>
  <div class="formula-row"><div class="fname">Structural gaps</div>
    <div class="fexpr">Prepositions, articles, conjunctions, pronouns, verb forms</div>
    <div class="fnote">Answered by grammar — tense, agreement, voice, fixed patterns.</div></div>
  <div class="formula-row"><div class="fname">Meaning gaps</div>
    <div class="fexpr">Verbs, nouns and adjectives chosen for sense and collocation</div>
    <div class="fnote">Answered by the paragraph’s meaning and by what normally partners the neighbouring words.</div></div>
</div>

<h2>The method</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Read the passage whole, gaps and all.</strong> You must know what it is saying before any gap means anything.</li>
<li><strong>For each gap, ask what part of speech fits.</strong> A gap after “the” and before a verb needs an adjective or noun; a gap after a subject and an auxiliary needs a verb form.</li>
<li><strong>Check the tense and number of the passage</strong> before choosing any verb — most cloze verb errors are tense and agreement slips.</li>
<li><strong>Read the completed sentence back.</strong> The ear catches wrong collocations faster than the eye.</li>
</ol>

<div class="note note-exam"><span class="ico">🎯</span><div><b>Answers are always in the passage, never beside it</b>
<p>If a gap needs a preposition, the phrase pattern will tell you which one. If it needs an adjective, the noun it describes will tell you the meaning. The options are four possibilities; the paragraph has already chosen one.</p></div></div>

<h2>High-frequency gap words</h2>
<table class="ledger" style="margin:1.2em 0">
  <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>What to listen for</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><b>Prepositions</b></td><td>The verb or adjective that governs it</td><td>consist <em>of</em> · depend <em>on</em> · prevented <em>from</em> · capable <em>of</em></td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Linking words</b></td><td>The logic between clauses</td><td><em>although</em> (contrast) · <em>therefore</em> (result) · <em>because</em> (cause) · <em>unless</em> (condition)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Verb forms</b></td><td>Tense, auxiliary, voice</td><td><em>has been</em> (present perfect passive) · <em>was</em> · <em>being</em></td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Determiners</b></td><td>Countability and number</td><td><em>few</em> (countable) vs <em>little</em> (uncountable) · <em>many</em> vs <em>much</em></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Practice cloze</h2>
<div class="example">
  <div class="example-h"><span class="num">B</span> Read the passage with its gaps, then answer the questions
  </div>
  <div class="example-b">
    <div class="step"><div class="step-l">Passage</div>
    <p style="margin-top:.6em">Kola nuts (1)____ played a part in West African ceremonies for centuries. No visitor is (2)____ without being offered one, and no important negotiation is (3)____ until the nuts have been shared. The nut is bitter, (4)____ few who receive it actually eat it; the point is the offering, not the taste. In the cities the custom has (5)____, as younger generations prefer soft drinks, but it (6)____ a courtesy that few would refuse outright. When a man carries kola to his in-laws, he is not buying anything; he (7)____ respect, and the gesture needs no (8)____.</p></div>
    <div class="step"><div class="step-l">Worked example — gap 3</div>
    The blank follows “is” and precedes “until the nuts have been shared”. A passive verb is required, and the perfect form matches “has been shared”: <b>begun</b>. (“Begun” is the past participle — “is begun”.)</div>
    <div class="step"><div class="step-l">Worked example — gap 4</div>
    The first clause says the nut is bitter, the second says people do not eat it. The ideas contrast, so the linker must show contrast: <b>although</b>.</div>
    <div class="ans">1 have · 2 welcomed · 3 begun · 4 although · 5 declined · 6 remains · 7 shows · 8 explanation</div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="note note-warn"><span class=\"ico\">⚠</span><div><b>Begun, began, beginning — know all three</b>
<p>Cloze papers adore this verb: <strong>began</strong> (past), <strong>begun</strong> (past participle, after <em>has/have/had/is/was</em>) and <strong>beginning</strong> (the -ing form). After “is”, “was”, “has been” the past participle <strong>begun</strong> is required. The same trap exists for <em>done/did</em>, <em>gone/went</em>, <em>taken/took</em> and <em>written/wrote</em>.</p></div></div>

<div class="note note-tip"><span class=\"ico\">💡</span><div><b>Tense harmony</b>
<p>If the passage is telling a story in the past (“played… was… continued”), a gap inside that story takes a past form too. If it is stating a general truth (“soil is… erosion strips…”), keep the present. The tense of the neighbouring verbs is the strongest clue in the whole cloze.</p></div></div>

<h2>Chapter recap</h2>
<ul>
<li>Read the whole passage first — the paragraph explains every gap.</li>
<li>Decide the part of speech, then the tense and number, then the meaning.</li>
<li>Prepositions come from the governing verb or adjective; linkers from the logic.</li>
<li>Watch the irregular participles: begun, done, gone, taken, written.</li>
<li>Read the finished sentence aloud in your head — collocation errors jump out.</li>
</ul>
`,
  quiz: [
    { q: 'In the practice passage, gap 1 is best filled by', opts: ['has', 'have', 'having', 'had'], a: 1, why: '“Kola nuts” is plural, and the sentence is present perfect: “have played”.' },
    { q: 'Gap 2 — “No visitor is ____ without being offered one” — requires', opts: ['welcoming', 'welcome', 'welcomed', 'welfare'], a: 2, why: 'A passive participle after “is”: the visitor is welcomed.' },
    { q: 'Gap 3 — “no important negotiation is ____ until the nuts have been shared” — is best filled by', opts: ['began', 'begin', 'begun', 'beginning'], a: 2, why: 'After “is”, with a perfect sense matching “have been shared”, the participle begun is required.' },
    { q: 'Gap 4 links two contrasting ideas, so it needs', opts: ['because', 'so', 'although', 'unless'], a: 2, why: 'Bitter nut versus people not eating it — contrast requires although.' },
    { q: 'Gap 5 — “In the cities the custom has ____” — means the custom has', opts: ['declined', 'declared', 'declined in value only', 'declination'], a: 0, why: '“Has declined” = has faded; the perfect tense needs the past participle.' },
    { q: 'Gap 6 — “but it ____ a courtesy” — takes', opts: ['remained', 'remaining', 'remains', 'remainder'], a: 2, why: 'The sentence is present tense: “it remains a courtesy”.' },
    { q: 'Gap 7 — “he ____ respect” — requires a present-tense verb', opts: ['shows', 'showed', 'showing', 'shown'], a: 0, why: 'Third person singular, present: “he shows”.' },
    { q: 'Gap 8 — “the gesture needs no ____” — is best filled by', opts: ['explanation', 'permission', 'invitation', 'translation'], a: 0, why: 'The gesture speaks for itself — it needs no explanation.' },
    { q: 'A cloze passage is best approached by first', opts: ['Answering each gap in order without reading on', 'Reading the whole passage to grasp its meaning', 'Ignoring the passage and using grammar alone', 'Choosing the longest option each time'], a: 1, why: 'The paragraph supplies the context that fixes every gap.' },
    { q: 'Before choosing a verb for a gap you should check the passage’s', opts: ['Length', 'Tense and number', 'Title', 'Punctuation'], a: 1, why: 'Tense harmony and agreement decide the form.' },
    { q: 'The gap in “He was prevented ____ leaving early” takes', opts: ['to', 'from', 'of', 'for'], a: 1, why: 'Prevent is followed by from + -ing.' },
    { q: '“The committee ____ divided in its views” takes', opts: ['is', 'are', 'be', 'being'], a: 1, why: 'When members are divided, British usage takes the plural verb: “are divided”.' },
    { q: 'The gap in “Neither of the boys ____ ready” takes', opts: ['are', 'is', 'were', 'have'], a: 1, why: 'Neither is singular — the verb is singular even with of + plural noun.' },
    { q: 'The linker “therefore” introduces a clause of', opts: ['Contrast', 'Result', 'Cause', 'Condition'], a: 1, why: 'It marks the consequence of what was just stated.' },
    { q: '“Unless you hurry, you ____ the bus” takes', opts: ['will miss', 'missed', 'would miss', 'missing'], a: 0, why: 'In a real condition, the main clause takes the future: “will miss”.' },
    { q: 'The gap in “The meeting was ____ because of the rain” requires', opts: ['postponed', 'postponing', 'postpone', 'postponement'], a: 0, why: 'After “was”, a past participle forms the passive: was postponed.' },
    { q: '“Little ____ known about the site” requires', opts: ['is', 'are', 'was', 'were'], a: 0, why: 'When little begins the clause, inversion follows: “Little is known…”.' },
    { q: 'In a cloze, a gap followed by a noun and preceded by “the” usually takes a/an', opts: ['Adverb', 'Adjective', 'Conjunction', 'Preposition'], a: 1, why: 'Determiner + adjective + noun is the normal English order.' },
    { q: 'The best collocation for “conduct” is', opts: ['conduct a research', 'conduct an experiment', 'conduct the music quickly', 'conduct a money'], a: 1, why: 'Conduct means to carry out — an experiment, a survey, a campaign.' },
    { q: '“Few” and “little” differ in that few is used with', opts: ['Uncountable nouns', 'Countable nouns', 'Adjectives', 'Verbs'], a: 1, why: 'Few people/things; little water/time.' }
  ]
});

JAMB.addChapter('english', {
  id: 'synthesis-ideas',
  title: 'Main Idea, Summary, Tone and Purpose',
  blurb: 'Distinguishing the topic from the main idea, judging a writer’s tone, and choosing the best title or summary.',
  time: '45 min',
  objectives: [
    'Distinguish topic from main idea and from supporting detail',
    'Choose the best title for a passage or paragraph',
    'Judge tone and attitude from the writer’s word choices',
    'Identify the writer’s purpose: to inform, persuade, entertain, warn or criticise',
    'Select the option that best synthesises a passage’s ideas'
  ],
  body: `
<p class="lead">These questions ask you to stand back from the passage and describe it: <strong>what is it about, what does the writer think, and why did they write it?</strong> They have a reliable method, and the method is the same every time — collect the evidence words, then match them to the options.</p>

<h2>Topic, main idea, detail</h2>
<div class="formula-box">
  <div class="fb-head">Three levels of statement</div>
  <div class="formula-row"><div class="fname">Topic</div>
    <div class="fexpr">A noun phrase — the subject: “soil fertility decline”</div></div>
  <div class="formula-row"><div class="fname">Main idea</div>
    <div class="fexpr">A full sentence — what is claimed about the subject: “soil decline persists because cheap remedies are missing”</div></div>
  <div class="formula-row"><div class="fname">Detail</div>
    <div class="fexpr">A single fact used as evidence — real, but too small to summarise the passage</div></div>
</div>

<div class="note note-exam"><span class="ico">🎯</span><div><b>The “yes, but…” test</b>
<p>Take each option and ask: is it true, and does it cover the <em>whole</em> passage? A detail option is true but too narrow. An extreme option covers too much. The main idea is the one that answers “yes, and yes” to both halves.</p></div></div>

<h2>Choosing a title or best summary</h2>
<ul>
<li>A good title is <strong>general enough</strong> to cover every paragraph and <strong>specific enough</strong> not to cover a different passage on the same subject.</li>
<li>A good summary keeps the writer’s <strong>claim</strong>, not just the writer’s <strong>examples</strong>. Options that list examples usually lose to the option that states the point the examples prove.</li>
<li>Watch the balance: if the passage is half history and half proposal, the title or summary must contain the proposal.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Tone and attitude</h2>
<p>Tone is decided by <strong>word choice (diction)</strong>. Collect the loaded words, decide whether each is positive, negative or neutral, and the tone names itself:</p>

<table class="ledger" style="margin:1.2em 0">
  <thead><tr><th>Tone</th><th>Means</th><th>Signalled by</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><b>Informative / objective</b></td><td>Reporting without judging</td><td>Neutral nouns, numbers, no adjectives of feeling</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Cautionary / warning</b></td><td>Pointing out danger</td><td>however · unless · risks · consequence</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Satirical / critical</b></td><td>Mocking or blaming</td><td>Irony, exaggerated praise, biting adjectives</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Nostalgic</b></td><td>Regretting a lost past</td><td>once · no longer · in those days · faded</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Ambivalent</b></td><td>Of two minds</td><td>on the one hand… on the other · yet · however</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Admiring / eulogistic</b></td><td>Praising</td><td>generous · remarkable · selfless</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="note note-warn"><span class=\"ico\">⚠</span><div><b>Neutral is the default trap</b>
<p>Passages with any “however”, “yet” or “unfortunately” are <strong>not</strong> neutral — the writer has taken a position. “Informative” is correct only where the writer genuinely reports without evaluating.</p></div></div>

<h2>Writer’s purpose</h2>
<div class="formula-box">
  <div class="fb-head">Why was this written?</div>
  <div class="formula-row"><div class="fname">To inform / explain</div>
    <div class="fexpr">Facts, process, cause and effect; neutral diction</div></div>
  <div class="formula-row"><div class="fname">To persuade / argue</div>
    <div class="fexpr">Should · must · must not · appeals; the writer’s side is visible</div></div>
  <div class="formula-row"><div class="fname">To warn / caution</div>
    <div class="fexpr">Risks, consequences, “unless” clauses, recommendations</div></div>
  <div class="formula-row"><div class="fname">To entertain / amuse</div>
    <div class="fexpr">Humour, anecdote, surprise, light diction</div></div>
  <div class="formula-row"><div class="fname">To criticise / satirise</div>
    <div class="fexpr">Irony, mock praise, exaggerated faults</div></div>
</div>

<h2>Practice passage</h2>
<div class="example">
  <div class="example-h"><span class="num">C</span> Read the passage, then answer the questions
  </div>
  <div class="example-b">
    <div class="step"><div class="step-l">Passage</div>
    <p style="margin-top:.6em">Nobody who watches children play can doubt that sport is good for them. The running, the shouting, the sheer delight of a game invented on the spot — all of it speaks to something essential. Yet the same passion, commercialised and compressed into trophies and scholarships, can curdle surprisingly quickly. Parents who once urged their children to enjoy the game begin to keep score of the medals. Coaches who once taught teamwork start selecting for winners alone. The child who loses is no longer simply a child who lost a game; she is a failure, and she remembers being one for a long time. None of this argues against sport. It argues, rather, for a careful watch over the adults who profit — financially or emotionally — from a child’s success.</p></div>
    <div class="step"><div class="step-l">Worked example</div>
    <b>Question:</b> The writer’s attitude towards children’s sport is —
    <p style="margin-top:.5em"><b>A.</b> wholly opposed · <b>B.</b> positive but watchful · <b>C.</b> indifferent · <b>D.</b> bitterly sarcastic</p></div>
    <div class="step"><div class="step-l">Method</div>
    The opening sentence praises sport; “yet” introduces the danger; the last sentence says the argument is <em>not against</em> sport but against profit-driven adults. Only <b>B</b> holds both halves.</div>
    <div class="ans">B — tone options that drop half the passage are always wrong</div>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>Chapter recap</h2>
<ul>
<li>Topic = subject; main idea = the claim about it; detail = one piece of evidence.</li>
<li>The best title covers the whole passage; the best summary keeps the claim, not the examples.</li>
<li>Tone is read off the loaded words: positive, negative or neutral.</li>
<li>Purpose: inform, persuade, warn, entertain, criticise — decided by modals and diction.</li>
<li>Reject any option that ignores half the passage.</li>
</ul>
`,
  quiz: [
    { q: 'In the practice passage, the child who loses is described as remembering her failure “for a long time” in order to show that', opts: ['Children have poor memories', 'The effect of adult pressure is lasting', 'Losing is educational', 'Coaches are strict'], a: 1, why: 'The lasting hurt is the writer’s evidence that the pressure does harm.' },
    { q: 'The word “curdle” in the passage suggests that passion can', opts: ['Turn sour', 'Grow stronger', 'Be bottled', 'Be shared'], a: 0, why: 'Curdle literally means turn sour — used figuratively for spoiling.' },
    { q: 'The writer’s purpose in the passage is mainly to', opts: ['Entertain with anecdotes of sport', 'Warn against adult influence in children’s sport', 'Advertise scholarships', 'Explain the rules of games'], a: 1, why: 'The final sentence frames the passage as a caution about profiting adults.' },
    { q: 'The best title for the practice passage is', opts: ['The Joys of Running', 'Keeping Score: Adults in Children’s Sport', 'How to Coach Winners', 'A History of Trophies'], a: 1, why: 'It covers both the praise of sport and the warning about adults.' },
    { q: 'The tone of the practice passage is best described as', opts: ['Wholly negative', 'Positive but cautionary', 'Neutral and factual', 'Furious'], a: 1, why: 'The writer loves sport yet warns about its commercialised side.' },
    { q: 'A supporting detail differs from the main idea in that it', opts: ['States the central claim', 'Serves as evidence for the claim', 'Contradicts the claim', 'Summarises the whole passage'], a: 1, why: 'Details prove the main idea; they do not replace it.' },
    { q: 'Which word would LEAST suggest a cautionary tone?', opts: ['However', 'Unless', 'Consequently', 'Fortunately'], a: 3, why: 'Fortunately signals relief, not warning; the others introduce risk or consequence.' },
    { q: '“On the one hand… on the other hand…” most strongly suggests a tone that is', opts: ['Satirical', 'Ambivalent', 'Admiring', 'Objective'], a: 1, why: 'The writer is weighing two sides.' },
    { q: 'A passage full of figures, dates and no judging adjectives is most likely', opts: ['Persuasive', 'Informative', 'Satirical', 'Nostalgic'], a: 1, why: 'Neutral reporting diction marks an informative passage.' },
    { q: 'A main-idea option that reads like a list of the passage’s examples is usually', opts: ['The correct answer', 'A distractor — too narrow', 'Too general', 'The writer’s purpose'], a: 1, why: 'Examples are details; the main idea states what they prove.' },
    { q: 'The phrase “a slow emergency” shows that a writer can create tone by', opts: ['Using an oxymoron-like pairing', 'Changing the passage length', 'Avoiding adjectives', 'Asking questions'], a: 0, why: 'Two words pulling in opposite directions produce an effect — a deliberate tonal choice.' },
    { q: 'The writer’s attitude is best judged by', opts: ['The passage’s length', 'The loaded word choices', 'The number of paragraphs', 'The final punctuation'], a: 1, why: 'Diction carries attitude.' },
    { q: '“It argues, rather, for a careful watch over the adults” tells us the writer is', opts: ['Calling for surveillance of children', 'Recommending caution about adults who profit', 'Attacking all coaches', 'Supporting scholarships'], a: 1, why: 'The sentence redirects the criticism from sport to profiting adults.' },
    { q: 'A sentence that states the passage’s central claim is called the', opts: ['Topic sentence', 'Topic', 'Figure of speech', 'Cloze sentence'], a: 0, why: 'The topic sentence carries the main idea of its paragraph.' },
    { q: 'A best-summary option must be', opts: ['Shorter than the passage and cover every part of it', 'The longest option available', 'A direct quotation', 'Free of the writer’s opinion'], a: 0, why: 'Compression without loss of coverage.' },
    { q: 'A passage that mixes praise with “yet” and “however” is unlikely to be', opts: ['Ambivalent', 'Wholly neutral', 'Cautionary', 'Critical'], a: 1, why: 'The turn words show the writer has taken a side.' },
    { q: 'A writer who describes a town’s past prosperity before its present decay is most likely', opts: ['Nostalgic', 'Satirical', 'Objective', 'Playful'], a: 0, why: 'Contrasting a lost better past with a worse present is nostalgia’s signature.' },
    { q: '“None of this argues against sport” functions to', opts: ['Reverse the reader’s sympathy', 'Prevent the passage being read as wholesale opposition', 'Introduce a new topic', 'End the argument'], a: 1, why: 'It limits the criticism — sport stays good; only the adult influence is attacked.' }
  ]
});

